package testing

// Package testing holds Site, the place in the source a call was written.
// A program imports it as
//
//     import testing from "std/testing"
//
// and the compiler reads it from $TACK_HOME/src/std/testing, or
// ~/.tack/src/std/testing, as it reads std/bytes. It is written in Tack
// alone, with no C, and imports no other std package, so that any std
// package's tests can import it without an import cycle.
//
// A function or a method may declare one parameter of type Site, which a
// call never writes an argument for: the compiler passes the call's own
// place, worked out when it compiles the call, so nothing is read at run
// time. A call written in a function that has a Site parameter of its
// own passes that one on instead, so a helper that declares a
// `_ testing.Site` reports the place its own caller was written:
//
//     func expect(_ testing.Site, ok bool) { ... }
//
//     func expect_both(_ testing.Site, a bool, b bool) {
//         expect(a)
//         expect(b) // both pass the place expect_both was called from
//     }
//
// A call in a closure's body passes its own place: a closure has no Site
// parameter. A function with a Site parameter is only ever called by
// name or as a method: it is no function value, satisfies no interface
// method and is not extern; no closure, function type or interface
// method has a Site parameter, nor does main, which has no parameters; a
// Site parameter is never mut; and a variadic parameter's values are
// never Sites. A parameter declared as Site is always filled in by the
// call, so a Site value is never written as an argument to one, but it
// is a value like any other everywhere else: a field may hold it, a
// function may return it, and a generic function's parameter of a type
// parameter may take it.

// Site is where a call was written, as a diagnostic at the call shows it.
// file is the name the compiler's diagnostics give the call's file; line
// and col are the line and the column of the call's first byte, both
// counted from 1, the column in bytes, a tab counting as one; and excerpt
// is what a diagnostic at the call prints below its header: the line
// before the call's first line, that line and, under it, a marker as
// long as the call, and the line after, each numbered and ended by a
// newline, the lines that exist of the three.
pub type Site struct {
    pub file    string
    pub line    int64
    pub col     int64
    pub excerpt string
}
